      "math_academy_id": "q-346698",
      "sequence_position": 12,
      "problem": "Consider the area under the curve $y=2{x}^{2}-1$ over the interval $[1,5]$ using a left Riemann sum with step size $Δx=1.$\n\nThe estimated area under the curve $y=2{x}^{2}-1$ over the interval $[1,5]$ is {{field-1}}.\nSince the function is strictly {{field-2}}\nin the interval,  the approximation is an\n{{field-3}}\nof the actual area.",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14221930/assets/671d5efc8abc559cf638bfbfdda76bddb0b8d5c2d9134dffbbf7eab0e2f0b25a.png)\n\nThe area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $7$ | $17$ | $31$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(1+7+17+31)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =56\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =56\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is strictly $\\text{increasing}$ over the interval $[1,5],$ the rectangles reach *below* the function, which means our approximation is an $\\text{underestimate}$ of the actual area.",
